# 1) Short summary (why both)

* SEO = long-term organic visibility across search engines (build authority with people-first content, technical health, and backlinks). ([Google for Developers][1])
* GEO (local targeting / local SEO / geotargeted ads) = quick, highly relevant reach to nearby users (maps, “near me” searches, ads targeted by location). Great for events, in-person offers, and driving local signups. ([Google for Developers][2], [Aillum][3])

# 2) Five quick wins (do these in the next 7–14 days)

1. Claim + fully complete your Google Business Profile (name, hours, photos, services, short description). People hit maps first. ([Google for Developers][2])
2. Add LocalBusiness / Organization schema (JSON-LD) to your homepage and location pages (address, phone, opening hours). Easy for Google to understand you. ([Google for Developers][4])
3. Publish one “people-first” page that answers a high-value query for your audience (FAQ or short guide) using plain helpful language — not keyword stuffing. (Google rewards helpful content.) ([Google for Developers][1])
4. Fix any obvious speed/usability issues on your main pages (mobile load, CLS, LCP) — aim for decent Core Web Vitals. It improves user experience and removes friction. ([Google for Developers][5])
5. Create a single local landing page (if applicable): city/neighbourhood + what you do (e.g., “Hyperfocus Zone — ADHD-friendly coworking & workshops in Cardiff”) and link it in your nav/footer.

# 3) 90-Day Roadmap (priority by week blocks)

Weeks 1–2: Foundations

* Claim Google Business Profile + Apple Business Connect if you need Apple Maps. Add photos & posts. ([Google for Developers][2])
* Add Local schema & organization markup sitewide. Test with Google Rich Results test. ([Google for Developers][4])
* Run a crawl (Screaming Frog / Sitebulb / Ahrefs) to find broken links, duplicate titles, missing meta.

Weeks 3–6: Content & Local Signals

* Publish 4 focused pages: 2 flagship “people-first” guides (global SEO), 2 hyperlocal pages (one per key city/neighbourhood). Use real user questions as headings. ([Google for Developers][1])
* Build short, authentic review-gathering flow: ask happy users to leave Google reviews (link via short URL in email/Discord). Reviews boost local trust. ([Google for Developers][2])

Weeks 7–12: Technical & Link Signals

* Improve Core Web Vitals for top pages: compress images, use lazy loading, minimize main-thread JS. Measure with PageSpeed Insights / Search Console. ([Google for Developers][5], [Seologist][6])
* Outreach for 8–12 local / niche backlinks (collabs, guest posts, local community sites). Authority links = ranking fuel. ([Google for Developers][7])

# 4) GEO (Ad + Local Targeting) plan — quick playbook

* Geo-targeted search & social ads: run small A/B tests by radius (e.g., 5 km, 20 km) around key locations and compare CPL (cost per lead). Use ad copy with local keywords and a clear CTA (book, join Discord, RSVP). ([Aillum][3])
* Use location-based promos (workshop discount, first-visit free, “Hyperfocus Hour”) and measure redemptions.
* Combine with Local Landing Pages → reduce friction / increase conversion.

# 5) Technical checklist (prioritise)

* Search Console & Analytics installed (start tracking). ([Google for Developers][8])
* XML sitemap + robots.txt (submit sitemap to Search Console). ([Google for Developers][7])
* Mobile-friendly/responsive design. ([Google for Developers][5])
* Structured data for LocalBusiness/Organization and Events (if you run meetups). ([Google for Developers][4])
* Canonical tags and proper hreflang if you target multiple languages/regions. ([Google for Developers][9])

# 6) Content plan (types that work for Hyperfocus Zone)

* Evergreen guide: “How to set up a hyperfocus-friendly workspace” — long form (1.5–3k words) with visuals and checklists. ([Google for Developers][1])
* Local events pages: meetups, workshops, classes — schema-tagged. ([Google for Developers][4])
* Microcontent for Discord/Patreon: short tips, audio notes, repurpose to blog snippets (good for engagement & internal linking).
* Testimonials + case studies (short video + text) — useful for trust and rich snippets.

# 7) KPIs to watch (measure weekly / monthly)

* Organic sessions & top landing pages (GA4).
* Google Business Profile views / direction requests / calls. ([Google for Developers][2])
* Rankings for target keywords (global) and local phrases (e.g., “ADHD coworking \[city]”).
* Core Web Vitals metrics & mobile bounce rate. ([Google for Developers][5])
* Conversion rate on local landing pages (signups, bookings, Discord joins).

# 8) Tools I recommend

* Free: Google Search Console, PageSpeed Insights, Google Business Profile (free to claim), Lighthouse. ([Google for Developers][5])
* Paid / freemium: Ahrefs / Semrush (keyword + backlink work), LocalFalcon (local rank tracking), Zapier to automate review requests.

# 9) Practical example: Local landing page structure (copy skeleton)

Title: Hyperfocus Zone — ADHD-friendly coworking & workshops in \[City]
H1: Hyperfocus Zone \[City] — focus-friendly space + workshops
Intro (2 lines): what you offer + 1 local trust signal (hours, neighborhood).
Sections: Services → Workshops → How to book (CTA) → Reviews → Map + address (schema) → FAQs (answer real user questions).
Make the CTA big and local: “Book your free drop-in in \[City]” — button.

# 10) Risks & where to avoid wasting time

* Don’t obsess over tiny Core Web Vitals score tweaks if your content or UX is poor — first improve clarity and usefulness, then polish performance. ([Stan Ventures][10], [Google for Developers][1])
* Avoid duplicate local pages with thin content — each location page must be real, unique, and useful. ([Google for Developers][4])

